                <pb facs="0585" n="471" sameAs="frus:frus1922v02/pg471"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">838.00/1875: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Haiti</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Dunn</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-05-12T16:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 12, 1922—4
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>40. For General Russell:</p>
                <p>Your despatch No. 24, April 28.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d394-f1"/></p>
                <p>The Haitian Minister has made representations to the Secretary of State, setting
                    before him the documents of which copies were transmitted by you, regarding the
                    eligibility of Monsieur Borno. He was told that in view of the decision of the
                    Council of State regarding Borno’s eligibility, which decision is apparently not
                    subject to review by any other body, the question of eligibility must be
                    regarded as closed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
